<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Midhat Pasha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1822">1822</a>–<a href="/years/1884">1884</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ahmed Şefik Midhat Pasha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Midhat Pasha was an Ottoman statesman and reformer. As governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> province and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> from 1869 to 1872 he introduced the new provincial system, settled tribes, and founded schools, newspapers and public works. As grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> he was the main author of the Ottoman constitution of 1876, but Abdulhamid II exiled him, and he was murdered in prison at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Taif</a> in 1884.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/midhat-pasha.jpg" alt="Midhat Pasha" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Atelier Adèle · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Midhat_Pa%C5%9Fa_1877-1.1R_V01-1.1_cleaned.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
